meta {
  name: Upload Sold Document
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

put {
  url: {{soldDocumentUploadUrl}}
  body: text
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/pdf
}

body:text {
  %PDF-1.4
  1 0 obj<</Type/Catalog>>endobj
  trailer<</Root 1 0 R>>
  %%EOF
}

tests {
  test("storage accepts the upload", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  # PUT <uploadUrl> — the raw Card 5 upload (step 2 of 3, PAC-40)

  Not an API endpoint: this goes **straight to object storage** using the URL
  from `Presign Sold Document`.

  - **Auth:** `none` — deliberately. The collection-level bearer would be sent
    on this request and **break the S3 signature**; a presigned URL carries its
    own credentials. The web client uses a bare `fetch` here rather than
    `apiFetch` for exactly this reason.

  ## Headers
  `Content-Type` **must** match the signed value (`requiredHeaders` from the
  presign response) or storage rejects the PUT.

  ## Body
  A minimal valid PDF stub. The bytes are irrelevant to the API — only the
  object's **type and size**, which `POST /sold-deals` reads back with
  `HeadObject`. Because the server derives the size rather than trusting the
  declared one, this stub can be far smaller than the `size` sent at presign
  time and the chain still works.

  ## Response
  - `200 OK` — object stored; the key is now attachable.
  - `403 Forbidden` — signature mismatch: usually an `Authorization` header
    being sent, a differing `Content-Type`, or an expired URL.
}
